 signal execution_finished()
 
+# [MP8] Seat number → which coach_shoot_position_N animation to use.
+# Computed from the actual coordinates by _mp8_map_animations() in green_pea.gd and written in here (each peer computes it once,
+# because shoot_player is a call_local broadcast and every machine has to play the animation).
+# Empty table = not initialized, so the seat number itself is used (with ≤4 players the two are always identical, matching vanilla value for value).
+var mp8_anim_by_seat: Array = []
+
+
+func _mp8_anim_index(seat: int) -> int :
+
+	if seat >= 0 and seat < mp8_anim_by_seat.size():
+		return int(mp8_anim_by_seat[seat])
+
+	return seat
+
+# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+# [MP8] T55 — spread the stagger delay thinner by player count (2026-09-02, reported by a player on 09-01)
+# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+#
+# In the player's own words, "The new positions (the fifth and sixth person) pick up the fork much more slowly than the original four
+#            after the master picks the newspaper back up, which hurts game balance."
+#
+# Vanilla puts the `await` **inside the loop body** in both of these loops, like this
+#
+#     for player in players:
+#         player.continue_eating_rpc.rpc()
+#         await get_tree().create_timer(randf_range(0, 0.3)).timeout
+#
+# So player i only gets the command **once the random delays of the i players before them have all added up** —
+# expected 0.15×i s, worst case 0.3×i s. Double the player count and the last player's wait doubles too.
+#
+#     seat 1  0.00 s   seat 4  0.45 s (vanilla worst)
+#     seat 5  0.60 s   seat 6  0.75 s   seat 8  1.05 s   (averages, worst case ×2)
+#
+# On top of that comes the fixed 0.2 s + 0.3 s tween inside `continue_eating_rpc`,
+# so seat 8 can take up to 2.6 seconds from "the coach goes back to reading the paper" to "the fork is usable".
+# Meanwhile the coach side does `anim_tree_coach.set(...blend_amount, 0)` and goes back to reading **instantly** —
+# the visual cue appears for everyone at once, but the actual unlock trails off in a long tail. That is the "much slower" the player meant.
+#
+# ⚠️ Strictly speaking "total eating time" roughly cancels out (the pause loop uses the same order, so later players also get paused later
+#    and gain about that much eating time during the execution). But **the compensation happens during the execution, when nobody is eating**,
+#    while **the loss happens right as the round resumes, when every moment counts** — so the player's feel for it is correct.
+#
+# 🩸 Yet another case of "vanilla wrote it for 4 players, and the cost grows linearly with player count" (same category as item 55 in group D of section 6).
+#    This stagger is **intentional** (everyone snaps back one after another, which looks natural), so it can't be removed, only spread thinner.
+#
+# The approach is each step's delay = randf_range(0, total budget / player count), so **the length of the whole ramp doesn't depend on player count**.
+# With 4 players 1.2/4 = 0.3, identical value for value to vanilla's `randf_range(0, 0.3)` — for ≤4 players not a single character changed.
+#
+# ⚠️ This uses `players.size()`, not the living count. Both loops iterate the whole array (the `continue` one
+#    doesn't even skip dead players), so the ramp length is set by the array length and the living count would come out too short.
+const MP8_STAGGER_BUDGET: float = 1.2   # Vanilla's total ramp length for 4 players, 4 × 0.3
+
+
+func _mp8_stagger() -> float :
+
+	return randf_range(0.0, MP8_STAGGER_BUDGET / float(maxi(players.size(), 1)))
+
